---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

// The brief supplies no meta description for this page, so this is assembled
// only from phrasing already checked in the brief's approved copy — no new
// claims. Kept under ~155 chars so Google doesn't truncate it.
const DESCRIPTION =
	'Employee #1 and CTO at AutogenAI since the first commit in July 2022. Previously CTO at Policy in Practice, and five years in welfare-to-work.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About · ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={DESCRIPTION} />
		<style>
			main {
				width: 720px;
			}
			p {
				line-height: 1.65;
				margin-bottom: 1.1em;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>About</h1>

			<p>
				I'm employee #1 and CTO at AutogenAI. I've owned the product architecture, the
				engineering organisation and the technical strategy since the first commit in July 2022.
			</p>

			<p>
				AutogenAI builds LLM software for writing bids and proposals. We were live with our
				first customer about four months after the first commit, on GPT-3 completions, a few
				weeks before ChatGPT launched — and the architecture has survived four generations of
				model capability since, from retrieval and multi-model routing to evaluation tooling and
				agentic orchestration, without a rewrite. I took it from an empty repository through
				seed, Series A and a Series B co-led by Salesforce Ventures and Spark Capital, and built
				a team of forty across engineering, ML, product, platform, IT and compliance in the UK,
				US and Australia.
			</p>

			<p>
				Our customers are organisations that win their work by bidding for it: defence primes,
				outsourcers and government-services firms, the large consultancies, transport and
				infrastructure operators, and social-purpose providers running frontline services. They
				buy software the way regulated organisations buy software, so a fair amount of my job
				has been the assurance work that lets them say yes — FedRAMP High authorisation in
				January 2026, on top of SOC 2, ISO 27001, IRAP, Cyber Essentials Plus and Defence Cyber
				Certification.
			</p>

			<p>
				Before AutogenAI I was CTO at Policy in Practice, which sells welfare and benefits
				software, policy research and consultancy to local authorities, central government and
				the organisations delivering services for them. I built an in-house engineering team
				from nothing to take over from a departing outsourced supplier with no break in service,
				kept platforms serving tens of thousands of daily users healthy while the legacy systems
				underneath them were replaced, and recruited my own successor. Before that I was a Rails
				developer at Homeflow, and for six years I ran Nine Worlds, a 1,500-person residential
				convention I founded — brand, ticketing, e-commerce, operations, finance and a great
				deal of customer service.
			</p>

			<p>
				The first part of my career was in welfare-to-work. I spent five years at a provider
				designing, marketing and running government-funded employment programmes and writing the
				bids that won them, and came back later as interim business development director. In
				between I founded Indus Delta, the sector's newsletter and online community, which I
				sold to Inclusion in 2010, and spent several years self-employed building Drupal sites
				and advising on web strategy and community.
			</p>

			<p>
				I read Mathematics at Warwick and took an MSc in Computer Science at Birkbeck in 2022,
				by way of Makers Academy in 2015. Before all of it, two years with VSO teaching maths
				and physics in a locally run school in Central Africa — still the hardest job I have
				had.
			</p>

			<p><a href="mailto:dan@whitston.uk">dan@whitston.uk</a></p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
